  "subtitle": "A paper-and-plastic information system where a strip's position, tilt, and ink color are the data — and 'handoff' still means literally handing it to the next controller",
  "why_scout_cares": "The museum has almost no artifact where the interface is a physical procedure rather than a device: paper, a plastic holder, a metal rail board, and a pen. The air-traffic-control flight progress strip is exactly that, and it is the system that organized some of the most consequential real-time human-machine-human work of the late 20th century. Each flight is a strip of paper in a plastic 'strip holder,' slotted into a 'strip board' whose vertical rails divide into bays. The strip's content is data, but so is the strip's *arrangement*: approach and area controllers keep strips in altitude order, tower controllers use bays to represent the ground, runway, and air, and a strip deliberately offset from alignment ('cocked out') is a visible flag. Controllers annotate with colored pens that encode who issued which instruction, and the computerized word 'handoff' survives from the older practice of physically handing the strip to the next controller. It is a distributed, eyes-up, gesture-and-placement interface with no single-screen metaphor — and a direct ancestor of the electronic strips that tried, with difficulty, to reproduce the paper system in software.",
